{"id":"cluster-administration","corpus":"k8s","title":"Cluster Administration","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Cluster Administration"},{"kind":"p","text":"Lower-level detail relevant to creating or administering a Kubernetes cluster."},{"kind":"p","text":"The cluster administration overview is for anyone creating or administering a Kubernetes cluster. It assumes some familiarity with core Kubernetes concepts."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Planning a cluster"},{"kind":"p","text":"See the guides in Setup for examples of how to plan, set up, and configure Kubernetes clusters. The solutions listed in this article are called distros."},{"kind":"p","text":"Not all distros are actively maintained. Choose distros which have been tested with a recent version of Kubernetes."},{"kind":"p","text":"Before choosing a guide, here are some considerations:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Do you want to try out Kubernetes on your computer, or do you want to build a high-availability, multi-node cluster? Choose distros best suited for your needs."},{"kind":"li","text":"Will you be using a hosted Kubernetes cluster, such as Google Kubernetes Engine, or hosting your own cluster?"},{"kind":"li","text":"Will your cluster be on-premises, or in the cloud (IaaS)? Kubernetes does not directly support hybrid clusters. Instead, you can set up multiple clusters."},{"kind":"li","text":"If you are configuring Kubernetes on-premises, consider which networking model fits best."},{"kind":"li","text":"Will you be running Kubernetes on \"bare metal\" hardware or on virtual machines (VMs)?"},{"kind":"li","text":"Do you want to run a cluster, or do you expect to do active development of Kubernetes project code? If the latter, choose an actively-developed distro. Some distros only use binary releases, but offer a greater variety of choices."},{"kind":"li","text":"Familiarize yourself with the components needed to run a cluster."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Managing a cluster"},{"kind":"li","text":"Learn how to manage nodes."},{"kind":"li","text":"Read about Node autoscaling."},{"kind":"li","text":"Learn how to set up and manage the resource quota for shared clusters."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Securing a cluster"},{"kind":"li","text":"Generate Certificates describes the steps to generate certificates using different tool chains."},{"kind":"li","text":"Kubernetes Container Environment describes the environment for Kubelet managed containers on a Kubernetes node."},{"kind":"li","text":"Controlling Access to the Kubernetes API describes how Kubernetes implements access control for its own API."},{"kind":"li","text":"Authenticating explains authentication in Kubernetes, including the various authentication options."},{"kind":"li","text":"Authorization is separate from authentication, and controls how HTTP calls are handled."},{"kind":"li","text":"Using Admission Controllers explains plug-ins which intercepts requests to the Kubernetes API server after authentication and authorization."},{"kind":"li","text":"Admission Webhook Good Practices provides good practices and considerations when designing mutating admission webhooks and validating admission webhooks."},{"kind":"li","text":"Using Sysctls in a Kubernetes Cluster describes to an administrator how to use the sysctl command-line tool to set kernel parameters ."},{"kind":"li","text":"Auditing describes how to interact with Kubernetes' audit logs."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Securing the kubelet"},{"kind":"li","text":"Control Plane-Node communication"},{"kind":"li","text":"TLS bootstrapping"},{"kind":"li","text":"Kubelet authentication/authorization"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Optional Cluster Services"},{"kind":"li","text":"DNS Integration describes how to resolve a DNS name directly to a Kubernetes service."},{"kind":"li","text":"Logging an